    "text": "When a bicycle repair club opened at North School, its organisers expected to spend most afternoons fixing broken chains and brakes. Their first visitors, however, brought bicycles that needed only small adjustments. Many owners simply did not know where to start. The volunteers realised that explaining a repair could be more useful than completing it quickly.\n\nThe club now meets every Wednesday from four to half past five. Students must book a place because the workshop has only six stands. They bring their own bicycles, but tools are provided. A local mechanic visits twice a month to check difficult repairs. Students are told not to attempt any repair involving safety unless an experienced volunteer is present.\n\nSixteen-year-old Mira joined because she wanted to save money. At first, she asked a volunteer to do everything for her. The volunteer instead showed her how to identify a loose brake cable and asked her to explain each step before trying it. The repair took longer, but a month later Mira corrected the same problem at home without help. She now brings a notebook rather than asking someone to take over.\n\nThe organisers measure success by asking visitors to describe what they have learned. They do not count the number of bicycles repaired as their only result. A fast service might help more customers, but it would give students fewer chances to practise. The club also keeps damaged parts for demonstrations, although visitors sometimes mistake this collection for rubbish.\n\nNext term, the school hopes to offer a weekend session for families. This is still a proposal: the mechanic has not confirmed whether she can attend. For now, the organisers prefer a small club that teaches carefully to a large service that promises more than its volunteers can deliver.",
