    "id": "R3",
    "title": "When a green label changes the question",
    "level": "B2",
    "text": "A reusable cup seems an uncomplicated symbol of environmental responsibility. Unlike a disposable cup, it can accompany its owner for years. Yet deciding which cup has the smaller environmental impact requires more than comparing what happens after a single drink. Manufacturing, transport, washing and the number of actual uses all matter. A durable object may demand more resources at the beginning and justify that cost only through repeated use.\n\nThis does not mean that reusable products are a mistake. It means that the useful comparison concerns systems of behaviour rather than isolated objects. A cup washed efficiently and used every day belongs to a different system from an identical cup bought as a souvenir and left in a cupboard. The product is the same, while its environmental role is not. A label can describe a material accurately without predicting what its owner will do.\n\nOne university café learned this when it introduced a discount for customers bringing reusable cups. Purchases of branded cups increased, and photographs of them appeared frequently on social media. However, staff observations suggested that many buyers continued to request disposable cups. Some had forgotten their reusable ones; others were carrying bags and preferred not to transport a dirty cup. Buying an object had been easier than establishing the routine required to use it.\n\nThe café then tested a deposit scheme. Customers could borrow a cup and return it to any of three collection points. The scheme transferred washing from individuals to the café and reduced the need to remember a personal cup. It also introduced new demands: returns had to be collected, cups occasionally disappeared, and staff needed storage space. Managers therefore recorded return rates and washing arrangements instead of celebrating the number of cups distributed.\n\nIt would be misleading to declare the scheme universally superior on the basis of this account. The campus had short walking distances and a central kitchen. A café in a railway station might face different transport patterns and fewer returning customers. The relevant question is whether a particular arrangement achieves repeated use under its actual conditions, not whether the word reusable appears in its advertising.\n\nEnvironmental communication becomes less convenient when these qualifications are included. Simple labels are memorable, whereas conditional explanations require attention. Nevertheless, leaving out the conditions can encourage people to confuse a purchase with a completed environmental benefit. Better communication would explain what a product enables, what habits it requires and which outcomes have actually been measured. Such an explanation offers less certainty at the moment of sale, but it gives users a more practical role afterwards.",
