    "text": "The discovery of a bundle of letters can transform a historical account, but the absence of letters is more difficult to interpret. It may indicate that people did not write, that their papers were destroyed, that a collector considered them unimportant or simply that researchers have not yet looked in the right place. These possibilities are not interchangeable. An archive is a record of what survived and was selected as well as a source of information about the lives it appears to represent.\n\nA researcher examining the history of a coastal town encountered this problem when studying its fishing industry. The municipal archive contained detailed correspondence between boat owners and officials, alongside insurance documents and accounts of disputes. The voices of people who repaired nets or sold small quantities of fish were much harder to locate. Reading only the available letters could make the town’s economy appear to consist almost entirely of negotiations among property owners. That impression would reflect the archive’s composition as much as the historical distribution of work.\n\nThe researcher turned to photographs, household records and interviews conducted decades earlier. These sources expanded the picture but introduced their own difficulties. A photograph might show several people mending nets without explaining whether the work was paid. A household account might record an expense without naming the person who earned the money. An interview could preserve an experience absent from official records while being shaped by later events and the interviewer’s questions. The new material did not simply fill empty spaces with transparent facts.\n\nOne tempting response would be to treat official documents as biased and personal recollections as authentic. The researcher resisted this division. An official account can provide precise information that a later memory cannot, while a recollection can reveal a relationship that the official account was never designed to record. The issue is not to rank whole categories of sources once and for all, but to ask what question each source can help answer and how it came to take its present form.\n\nThe final exhibition used paired captions. One described what an object or document supported; the other identified a question it left unresolved. A payment record, for example, confirmed that a repair had been purchased but did not establish who performed it. A photograph suggested participation by several family members without proving that their tasks were equally valued. Some visitors found the captions frustrating, wanting the museum to provide a complete narrative rather than reminders of its limits.\n\nThat frustration deserves consideration. Public history cannot ask every visitor to become a professional source critic before understanding a display. Yet a coherent story can acknowledge uncertainty without collapsing into a list of doubts. The exhibition’s account of shared work remained clear, while its captions marked the points at which description became inference. Rather than pretending that the missing voices had been fully recovered, it showed why their absence mattered and what evidence might bring them closer.\n\nAn archive’s silences should therefore neither be ignored nor filled automatically with the story a researcher hopes to tell. They are reasons to widen the search and examine the processes that preserved some experiences while obscuring others. This approach offers no guarantee that every lost perspective can be reconstructed. It does, however, prevent the convenience of the surviving collection from becoming an unnoticed boundary around historical imagination.",
